# Generated by Cargo
# will have compiled files and executables
debug
target

# These are backup files generated by rustfmt
**/*.rs.bk

# MSVC Windows builds of rustc generate these, which store debugging information
*.pdb

# Generated by cargo mutants
# Contains mutation testing data
**/mutants.out*/

# RustRover
#  JetBrains specific template is maintained in a separate JetBrains.gitignore that can
#  be found at https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/Global/JetBrains.gitignore
#  and can be added to the global gitignore or merged into this file.
#
#  Ignored whole rather than partially. JetBrains' own .idea/.gitignore already
#  excludes workspace.xml, so committing the rest would share six files — and the
#  only one carrying settings enables ESLint, in a Rust workspace. Nothing in there
#  is worth the drift. What the project actually enforces lives in Cargo.toml's
#  lints and in `cargo xtask ci`, which is the same for every editor.
.idea/

# crates.io tokens. `cargo login` writes these to $CARGO_HOME, which is normally
# ~/.cargo — but this repository has its own .cargo/ for the xtask alias, and a
# CARGO_HOME pointed here would land a live token in a tracked directory. Ignored
# defensively: the cost is a line, the cost of the accident is a revoked token and
# a rewritten history.
.cargo/credentials
.cargo/credentials.toml

# The RustRover MCP endpoint. The port is assigned per machine, so a committed
# copy hands the next checkout a config that fails silently — which is worse than
# no config, because a dead MCP server looks like a missing feature.
.mcp.json

# The redkiln CLI, checked out here by the `backlog` CI job because redkiln is a
# private sibling repository rather than an npm package. It never exists in a
# normal working tree; it is ignored so that a contributor who clones it here to
# debug the job cannot stage a second repository by reflex.
.redkiln-cli/

# Agent scratch: settings, and `worktrees/` — full checkouts of this repository,
# each with its own `target/`, created when work is fanned out across isolated
# probes. Phases 1 and 2 both used that method and both left six behind.
#
# Ignored for the same reason as `.idea/` above: every phase's exit gate
# references a clean tree, and a phrase that is never true is not a gate. The
# sharper reason is here, though — `git add -A` in this repository would
# otherwise stage several gigabytes of build output and a second copy of every
# source file, and the branch refs that make the worktrees findable live in
# `.git/` regardless.
.claude/
